1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,631
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,651 --> 00:00:07,935
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:08,335 --> 00:00:14,495
This is Lecture 16, control of computerised embroidery machines.

4
00:00:14,895 --> 00:00:19,879
The pattern sewer from last time sews one piece at a time.

5
00:00:19,899 --> 00:00:24,279
Today's multi-head embroidery machine drives eight,

6
00:00:24,299 --> 00:00:27,557
even twenty heads from one main shaft,

7
00:00:27,577 --> 00:00:32,475
and one big frame carries every piece of fabric together.

8
00:00:32,875 --> 00:00:36,081
Two questions today. How fast can a big frame go?

9
00:00:36,101 --> 00:00:40,426
And when one head breaks a thread, what does the whole machine do,

10
00:00:40,446 --> 00:00:42,269
and are more heads worth it?

11
00:00:42,289 --> 00:00:47,141
Colour changes and design files are left to the slides and the assignment.

12
00:00:48,661 --> 00:00:50,201
Here's the problem.

13
00:00:50,601 --> 00:00:56,672
An embroidery shop runs an eight-head machine on jacket backs,

14
00:00:56,692 --> 00:01:01,781
and the design includes a 7-millimetre satin column.

15
00:01:02,181 --> 00:01:09,253
The frame has to complete each stitch move in a window of about 204 degrees,

16
00:01:09,273 --> 00:01:15,226
following a cycloidal law, with 800 newtons of drive force on Y.

17
00:01:15,626 --> 00:01:21,043
The design has 20 000 stitches at 800 r/min,

18
00:01:21,063 --> 00:01:28,086
and threads break 0.5 times per head per 10 000 stitches.

19
00:01:28,486 --> 00:01:31,706
Can the satin column run at 800?

20
00:01:32,106 --> 00:01:33,606
If not, how slow?

21
00:01:34,006 --> 00:01:39,666
And what are the machine's overall efficiency and pieces per hour?

22
00:01:40,066 --> 00:01:41,479
We'll answer at the end.

23
00:01:42,999 --> 00:01:45,343
A multi-head embroidery machine.

24
00:01:46,863 --> 00:01:47,963
Here's the structure.

25
00:01:48,363 --> 00:01:53,013
A row of heads hangs from one beam, and one main shaft,

26
00:01:53,033 --> 00:01:57,342
driven by the main servo, runs through all of them;

27
00:01:57,362 --> 00:02:02,097
under the table one hook shaft drives every head's hook.

28
00:02:02,497 --> 00:02:06,740
Each head's needle-bar case has 9 to 15 needle bars,

29
00:02:06,760 --> 00:02:11,577
but the shaft drives only the one lined up with the driver,

30
00:02:11,597 --> 00:02:14,037
the yellow one in the picture.

31
00:02:14,437 --> 00:02:21,719
The frame is one big frame across all heads, with a piece of fabric under each,

32
00:02:21,739 --> 00:02:26,711
carried through the same design by the X and Y servos.

33
00:02:27,111 --> 00:02:32,251
The shafts are shared, yet each head must be able to stop sewing on its own.

34
00:02:33,771 --> 00:02:37,938
The control layers resemble the pattern sewer's:

35
00:02:37,958 --> 00:02:41,601
the main controller reads the design file,

36
00:02:41,621 --> 00:02:45,091
sets speed and runs the colour sequence;

37
00:02:45,111 --> 00:02:51,109
motion control interpolates X and Y within the window by shaft angle.

38
00:02:51,509 --> 00:02:54,761
What's new is a head board on every head,

39
00:02:54,781 --> 00:02:58,591
handling the needle-bar jump solenoid, trimming,

40
00:02:58,611 --> 00:03:02,262
thread-break detection and the indicator lamp,

41
00:03:02,282 --> 00:03:07,449
so one head can stop sewing while the shared shafts keep turning.

42
00:03:07,849 --> 00:03:12,181
The operator mostly watches colour changes,

43
00:03:12,201 --> 00:03:16,229
rethreads broken heads and swaps frames.

44
00:03:16,629 --> 00:03:24,213
So output depends on speed, colour changes and trims, thread-break stops,

45
00:03:24,233 --> 00:03:25,463
and loading.

46
00:03:26,983 --> 00:03:28,923
How fast can a big frame go.

47
00:03:30,443 --> 00:03:32,249
As on a pattern sewer,

48
00:03:32,269 --> 00:03:37,312
the frame may move only in the window when the needle is out,

49
00:03:37,332 --> 00:03:41,546
which we borrow from Lecture 15: about 204 degrees.

50
00:03:41,946 --> 00:03:46,886
At 800 r/min a turn takes 75 milliseconds,

51
00:03:46,906 --> 00:03:51,611
so the window is only 42.5 milliseconds.

52
00:03:52,011 --> 00:04:01,626
Moving s along a cycloid, the peak acceleration is 2π s over T squared.

53
00:04:02,026 --> 00:04:14,266
At 800 r/min a 3-millimetre stitch needs 10.4 metres per second squared and a

54
00:04:14,286 --> 00:04:18,246
7-millimetre stitch 24.4.

55
00:04:18,646 --> 00:04:23,842
Acceleration goes with stitch length and with speed squared,

56
00:04:23,862 --> 00:04:29,057
so 10 percent more speed needs 21 percent more acceleration.

57
00:04:30,577 --> 00:04:38,037
This is one stitch's frame motion from the virtual lab.

58
00:04:38,437 --> 00:04:43,298
The green area is the window, needle out, frame free to move;

59
00:04:43,318 --> 00:04:47,859
the solid curve is position, the dashed one acceleration,

60
00:04:47,879 --> 00:04:53,140
and the red dashed line is what this eight-head frame can deliver.

61
00:04:53,540 --> 00:04:59,773
With a 7-millimetre stitch, watch the speed step from 600 up to 900:

62
00:04:59,793 --> 00:05:03,451
the window shrinks, the motion steepens,

63
00:05:03,471 --> 00:05:09,152
and the acceleration peak quickly breaks through the red line.

64
00:05:09,552 --> 00:05:14,349
On those stitches the frame is still moving after the needle enters:

65
00:05:14,369 --> 00:05:16,898
crooked stitches and broken needles.

66
00:05:18,418 --> 00:05:22,078
Now what the frame can deliver.

67
00:05:22,478 --> 00:05:29,089
Available acceleration is drive force minus friction, divided by moving mass.

68
00:05:29,489 --> 00:05:34,696
A multi-head frame spans every head, so more heads means a longer,

69
00:05:34,716 --> 00:05:35,805
heavier frame.

70
00:05:36,205 --> 00:05:45,204
The example uses the heavier Y axis, with mass 10 plus 4 per head in kilograms,

71
00:05:45,224 --> 00:05:51,825
800 newtons of drive and 50 of friction, all illustrative.

72
00:05:52,225 --> 00:05:59,148
With eight heads the frame is about 42 kilograms and gets 17.9 metres per

73
00:05:59,168 --> 00:06:00,575
second squared;

74
00:06:00,595 --> 00:06:05,045
with fifteen, about 70 kilograms and only 10.7.

75
00:06:05,445 --> 00:06:12,308
Same motors and drive: double the heads and you roughly halve the acceleration.

76
00:06:13,828 --> 00:06:20,147
Set required equal to available and solve for speed: n equals 204 over 6,

77
00:06:20,167 --> 00:06:24,576
times the root of available acceleration over 2π s,

78
00:06:24,596 --> 00:06:29,092
inversely proportional to the root of stitch length,

79
00:06:29,112 --> 00:06:31,697
just as for the pattern sewer.

80
00:06:32,097 --> 00:06:41,655
Here the horizontal axis is stitch length and the blue line the speed limit;

81
00:06:41,675 --> 00:06:48,586
the flat part on the left is the head's own 1100 r/min.

82
00:06:48,986 --> 00:06:53,886
Watch the curve fall as the heads go from 4 to 20.

83
00:06:54,286 --> 00:07:04,407
An eight-head machine manages about 1046 at 3 millimetres, 811 at 5,

84
00:07:04,427 --> 00:07:06,346
and 685 at 7.

85
00:07:07,866 --> 00:07:10,646
One head breaks a thread: what then.

86
00:07:12,166 --> 00:07:17,883
When one head breaks its needle thread, the whole machine stops,

87
00:07:17,903 --> 00:07:21,379
the frame backs up along the sewn path,

88
00:07:21,399 --> 00:07:27,026
and only the broken head stitches while the other bars stay up.

89
00:07:27,426 --> 00:07:29,606
Follow it along the stitch path.

90
00:07:30,006 --> 00:07:36,028
Between the actual break and the stop, d stitches have already been missed:

91
00:07:36,048 --> 00:07:37,639
the detection delay,

92
00:07:37,659 --> 00:07:44,405
made of the stitches needed to decide it's a break plus those turned while stopping;

93
00:07:44,425 --> 00:07:47,386
one patent's example totals about 10.

94
00:07:47,786 --> 00:07:50,704
The back-up b must be at least d,

95
00:07:50,724 --> 00:07:56,046
ideally two or three more so the mend overlaps the original.

96
00:07:56,446 --> 00:08:02,519
If b is less than d, the mend falls short and leaves a gap, a reject;

97
00:08:02,539 --> 00:08:07,906
if b is too large, it wastes time and the overlap gets thick.

98
00:08:08,306 --> 00:08:15,406
On the floor, a gap means too little back-up; a bump means too much.

99
00:08:16,926 --> 00:08:19,946
More heads cut both ways.

100
00:08:20,346 --> 00:08:26,762
This is the second page of the lab: sewing a 20 000-stitch design,

101
00:08:26,782 --> 00:08:28,907
with green for sewing,

102
00:08:28,927 --> 00:08:34,465
red for thread-break stops and purple for colour changes.

103
00:08:34,865 --> 00:08:42,313
Each head breaks 0.5 times per 10 000 stitches, independently and at random.

104
00:08:42,713 --> 00:08:49,453
Left, four heads; right, twenty; each sewn over and over.

105
00:08:49,853 --> 00:08:53,108
Any break stops the whole machine,

106
00:08:53,128 --> 00:08:58,984
so an H-head machine stops H times as often as a single head.

107
00:08:59,384 --> 00:09:02,644
Look how much more red there is on the right.

108
00:09:04,164 --> 00:09:08,495
When an arc-welding robot loses its arc mid-seam,

109
00:09:08,515 --> 00:09:15,244
the controller backs the torch up a little way along the path it has already

110
00:09:15,264 --> 00:09:16,132
travelled,

111
00:09:16,152 --> 00:09:20,483
restrikes the arc and overlaps the existing weld.

112
00:09:20,883 --> 00:09:26,938
That's the same idea as an embroidery frame backing up and mending to the break:

113
00:09:26,958 --> 00:09:33,166
the back-up must cover at least the distance travelled during the detection delay,

114
00:09:33,186 --> 00:09:34,457
or a gap remains.

115
00:09:34,857 --> 00:09:41,538
Put several robots in series on a paced line and any one stopping makes the

116
00:09:41,558 --> 00:09:42,968
whole line wait,

117
00:09:42,988 --> 00:09:48,597
so stoppages grow with the number of stations, just like heads.

118
00:09:48,997 --> 00:09:54,621
Either make each machine more reliable or make each recovery shorter.

119
00:09:56,141 --> 00:09:57,689
In everyday life,

120
00:09:57,709 --> 00:10:04,881
the closest thing to a multi-head embroidery machine is a metro train's doors.

121
00:10:05,281 --> 00:10:11,780
A train has many cars, and if any one door gets stuck, the whole train waits;

122
00:10:11,800 --> 00:10:15,421
the more cars, the more often it's delayed.

123
00:10:15,821 --> 00:10:21,441
That's exactly why any one head breaking a thread stops the whole machine.

124
00:10:21,841 --> 00:10:28,342
So the metro makes every door more reliable and clears problems faster,

125
00:10:28,362 --> 00:10:31,464
and so should the embroidery shop:

126
00:10:31,484 --> 00:10:35,781
lower the break rate and shorten each recovery.

127
00:10:37,301 --> 00:10:39,241
Back to the opening question.

128
00:10:39,641 --> 00:10:44,089
At 800 r/min the window is 42.5 milliseconds,

129
00:10:44,109 --> 00:10:51,138
and the 7-millimetre satin stitch needs 24.4 metres per second squared;

130
00:10:51,158 --> 00:10:58,981
the eight-head frame, at 42 kilograms, can give only 17.9, which is not enough.

131
00:10:59,381 --> 00:11:02,951
The limit is about 685 r/min,

132
00:11:02,971 --> 00:11:09,761
so the controller should slow down automatically there.

133
00:11:10,161 --> 00:11:19,370
To reach 850 the drive force would have to rise to about 1205 newtons.

134
00:11:19,770 --> 00:11:22,846
Efficiency: 8 expected breaks,

135
00:11:22,866 --> 00:11:30,483
each costing 40 seconds of handling plus about 1.6 of back-up and mending,

136
00:11:30,503 --> 00:11:36,366
about 333 seconds in all; six colour changes, 36 seconds;

137
00:11:36,386 --> 00:11:43,590
1500 seconds of sewing: about 80 percent, roughly 15.4 pieces an hour.

138
00:11:45,110 --> 00:11:48,644
Now Virtual lab 16-1, the multi-head embroidery machine.

139
00:11:48,664 --> 00:11:51,690
On the WenQuest platform you can do it yourself.

140
00:11:51,710 --> 00:11:55,688
The criteria on the right and the tasks below are blurred here,

141
00:11:55,708 --> 00:12:00,130
so you can read them on your own. Page one is frame and speed: on top,

142
00:12:00,150 --> 00:12:02,986
the frame's motion through one stitch; below,

143
00:12:03,006 --> 00:12:05,398
the speed limit against stitch length.

144
00:12:05,798 --> 00:12:09,797
First, pull the stitch length from 3 up to 7 millimetres.

145
00:12:09,817 --> 00:12:15,438
In the top chart the dashed acceleration climbs and breaks through the red line,

146
00:12:15,458 --> 00:12:19,458
and in the lower chart the red dot moves above the curve.

147
00:12:19,858 --> 00:12:25,854
Task one: at 7 millimetres, raise the heads from 8. The curve keeps falling.

148
00:12:25,874 --> 00:12:32,107
Work out the limit for fifteen heads with the formula, enter it below to check,

149
00:12:32,127 --> 00:12:34,481
then confirm with the sliders.

150
00:12:34,881 --> 00:12:41,839
Task two: back to eight heads, set the speed to 850 and raise the drive force.

151
00:12:41,859 --> 00:12:48,728
Check that the force from the answer slide is just enough for every criterion

152
00:12:48,748 --> 00:12:49,444
to pass,

153
00:12:49,464 --> 00:12:51,501
and record the minimum.

154
00:12:51,901 --> 00:12:55,638
Now push the stitch length past 12.1 millimetres.

155
00:12:55,658 --> 00:13:00,621
In the lower chart the dot enters the shaded Over DST limit zone.

156
00:13:00,641 --> 00:13:04,761
How such a stitch must be handled goes in your report.

157
00:13:05,161 --> 00:13:12,055
Page two is thread breaks and repair. On top is the timeline of one design,

158
00:13:12,075 --> 00:13:19,337
green for sewing, red for thread-break stops, purple for colour changes; below,

159
00:13:19,357 --> 00:13:22,103
efficiency against head count.

160
00:13:22,503 --> 00:13:27,783
Press Run again, and the breaks land somewhere different each time.

161
00:13:27,803 --> 00:13:30,473
Then raise the heads from 8 to 20,

162
00:13:30,493 --> 00:13:34,033
and the dot slides down the efficiency curve.

163
00:13:34,433 --> 00:13:38,402
Task three: set the detection delay to 6 stitches,

164
00:13:38,422 --> 00:13:41,752
then raise the back-up count step by step.

165
00:13:41,772 --> 00:13:46,618
How many leave no missed stitches without backing up too far?

166
00:13:46,638 --> 00:13:48,293
That's yours to find.

167
00:13:48,693 --> 00:13:50,727
Task four: on twenty heads,

168
00:13:50,747 --> 00:13:55,139
lower the break rate bit by bit and watch efficiency rise.

169
00:13:55,159 --> 00:13:58,105
How low must it go to reach 80 percent?

170
00:13:58,125 --> 00:14:01,604
Then try shortening the handling time instead,

171
00:14:01,624 --> 00:14:04,190
and compare which pays off better.

